WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Crowd: Good morning, Hank! It's Monday!

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.640
I woke up around 8:00 in Chicago on my friend Shannon's couch.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.960
Couch sleeping is the best sleeping.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.420
I borrowed Shannon's toothbrush,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.689
Sorry, Shannon. It was an emergency.

00:00:10.689 --> 00:00:12.029
restored the couch to its former glory,

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.660
and then hit the road. Why do I drive a station wagon? Cuz I'm a boss.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.859
I was headed to a book festival in Waupaca, Wisconsin, but first I had to drive through Chicago,

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:20.859
which has always been my favorite American city,

00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:26.399
a city of so many neighborhoods that you can never discover them all, a city as carl sandburg wrote, "under the

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:31.830
terrible burden of destiny laughing as a young man laughs." By the way, that place has great produce.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.200
I made it to the highway, and then drove north for three hours.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.240
Along the way, it all at once became fall.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.780
Then I finally made it to Waupaca, a city of six thousand people with three independent bookstores.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.840
A librarian had recommended I visit this place called Brainard's Bridge park,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.390
and so I headed straight there because I have found over the years that librarians make excellent recommendations.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:55.360
And the park was no exception. There was this beautiful river and this tree that refused to stop being a tree despite considerable challenges,

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.380
and then I found this walking path. Two roads diverged in a yellow wood and I,

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.820
I took the road more traveled by because I did not want to get lost in Waupaca, Wisconsin.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.150
I walked for a long time.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.099
It was really beautiful and there I was awash in the majesty of it all thinking:

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.110
I need to pee and I can't decide if I'm adequately in the woods right now to pee here or if I need to go

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.110
back to the park and find a bathroom.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.760
So...yeah.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.600
Just for the record, I ended up walking back to the park restroom.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.960
I'm gonna go ahead and wager that this person has Elvis flags for most of the major holidays.

00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:33.149
So I drove over to Waupaca high school to speak. "This is all voice over so you can say whatever you want", and someone gave

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.779
me a cheesehead which was nice. It was weird to talk to people after having spent 10 hours by myself,

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.900
but it always makes me really happy to see, for instance, a handmade

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.310
DFTBA shirt. I signed books for a while and then went to the amazing Waupaca public library's

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:50.849
amazing teen room where there is a poster of you on the wall and a space for gaming and a chandelier that played your music.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.710
(Hank's music)

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.199
Some teens and I played this incredibly complicated game of bunny murdering.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:01.060
And then I went to a fancy cocktail party for the festival where I met up with noted positive pranker Amy Krause Rosenthal

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.481
Who's organizing this event

00:02:02.481 --> 00:02:03.060
"When Amy?"

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.200
Amy: 10-10-10

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.210
Amy: At 10:10 p.m.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.520
at the Bean Sculpture in Chicago

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.553
It's a kind of sequel to 8-8-08. I'll be there playing a very minor role.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.740
If you want to learn how to participate click the screen, or read all about it in the doobly-doo.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.700
Anyway, it was a great party, but at one point a 91 year old woman got sick and an ambulance came and took her off to the hospital.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.140
For maybe half an hour after the ambulance came, it was quiet.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:33.760
And then it got louder, and then louder still as all of us, under the terrible burden of destiny, found a way to keep laughing.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.040
"Bedtime". Hank, I'll see you on Wednesday.

